on 1) needs to move left by 1 (swap with the 1 at position 0). The second 0 (position 3) needs to move left by 2 (swap with the 1 at position 2, then the 1 at position 1). So total swaps is 1 + 2 = 3.\n\nSo the approach is to iterate through each 0 in the original array, calculate how many 1s are to the left of it, and sum those numbers. That sum would be the total number of adjacent swaps required.\n\nSo, the steps would be:\n\n1. Iterate through the array, and for each 0, count how many 1s are to its left.\n2. Sum these counts.\n\nBut how to implement this in Python?\n\nLet me think. For each index i in the array, if the element is 0, then the number of 1s before i (i.e., from 0 to i-1) is the count. So, for each 0, we can calculate the number of 1s before it.\n\nBut how to do this efficiently. Let\'s think of an example. Suppose the array is [1, 0, 1, 0]. The 0s are at positions 1 and 3. For the first 0 at position 1, there is 1 1 before it (position 0). For the second 0 at position 3, there are 2 1s before it (positions 0 and 1). So total is 1 + 2 = 3.\n\nSo the algorithm would be:\n\nInitialize a counter for the number of 1s encountered so far. Then, for each element in the array, if it\'s a 0, add the current count of 1s to the total. Then, increment the count of 1s if the current element is 1.\n\nWait, but how does this work? Let\'s see:\n\nInitialize count_ones = 0, total_swaps = 0.\n\nFor each element in the array:\n\n- if element is 1, increment count_ones.\n- else (element is 0), add count_ones to total_swaps.\n\nYes, that seems correct. Because for each 0, the number of 1s to its left is the count_ones up to that point. So, for example, in the array [1, 0, 1, 0], the steps are:\n\n- First element is 1: count_ones becomes 1.\n- Second element is 0: add 1 to total_swaps (total is 1).\n- Third element is 1: count_ones becomes 2.\n- Fourth element is 0: add 2 to total_swaps (total is 3).\n\nWhich matches the example.\n\nSo the code would be:\n\ndef find_Min_