ement is 1, then next is 2. So, 1 is different from 2, so return 1. Which is correct.\n\nSo, the algorithm would be:\n\nLoop through the array from index 0 to len(arr)-1:\n\nFor each index i, check if arr[i] != arr[i+1]. If yes, return arr[i].\n\nBut wait, in the case where the array has only one element, like [5], then the loop would check i=0 and i+1 is 1, which is beyond the array. So, in that case, return arr[0].\n\nBut how to handle that? Well, the problem says that the array is sorted and has exactly one unique element. So, the array length is at least 1.\n\nSo, the code could be:\n\ndef search(arr):\n    for i in range(len(arr)-1):\n        if arr[i] != arr[i+1]:\n            return arr[i]\n    return arr[-1]\n\nWait, but in the case where the array is [1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 4], the loop runs from 0 to 4. For i=0, arr[0] is 1, arr[1] is 2. Different, return 1. Which is correct.\n\nAnother example: [1,1,2,2,3]. The loop checks i=0, arr[0] is 1, arr[1] is 1. Same. i=1, arr[1] is 1, arr[2] is 2. Different, return 1. Which is correct.\n\nAnother example: [3, 3, 3, 3, 3]. The loop would check each element. All are same. So the loop ends, and returns arr[-1], which is 3. Which is correct, since all are same, but the problem states that exactly one such integer exists. Wait, but in that case, the problem says that exactly one such integer exists. So the input would not be [3,3,3,3,3], because all are same. So the function is guaranteed to have exactly one unique element.\n\nSo, this approach works.\n\nBut wait, what if the unique element is at the end? Like [1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 4]. The loop would check i=0, arr[0] is 1, arr[1] is 2. Different, return 1. Which is correct.\n\nAnother case: [2, 2, 3, 3, 4]. The loop checks i=0, arr[0] is 2, arr[1] is 2. Same. i=1, arr[1] is 2, arr[2] is 3. Different, return 2. Which is correct.\n\nSo this approach works. Therefore, the code would be:\n\ndef search(arr):\n    for i in range(len(arr) - 1):\n        if arr[i] != arr[i + 1]:\n        