e each 0 needs to move past each 1 to its left. \n\nWait, but in the example above, the first 0 is at index 1. There is 1 1 to its left (the 1 at index 0). So that contributes 1. The second 0 is at index 3. The 1s to the left are the 1 at index 1. So that contributes 1. Total swaps 1+1=2? But earlier calculation said 3. Hmm, there's a discrepancy here. \n\nWait, maybe my initial example is wrong. Let me re-examine. Original array [1, 0, 1, 0]. Sorted array is [0, 0, 1, 1]. The steps to sort:\n\nOriginal: 1 0 1 0 → sorted: 0 0 1 1.\n\nTo get from original to sorted:\n\nFirst, move the 0 at position 1 to the left. That requires 1 swap (swap positions 1 and 0). Now array is [0, 1, 1, 0]. Then move the 0 at position 3 to the left. It needs to pass through the two 1s. Each swap moves it left by one. So after two swaps, it becomes [0, 1, 0, 1]. Then, the 0 at position 2 needs to move left. But wait, after the first two swaps, the array is [0, 1, 0, 1]. Then, the 0 at position 2 needs to move left. That would require two swaps (swap with position 1, then with position 0). So total swaps would be 1 + 2 + 2 = 5? But that seems high. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps the correct approach is to count the number of 1s to the left of each 0. \n\nIn the original array, the 0s are at positions 1 and 3. \n\nFor the first 0 (position 1), there is 1 1 to its left (position 0). \n\nFor the second 0 (position 3), there are 2 1s to its left (positions 0 and 1). \n\nTotal swaps would be 1 + 2 = 3. \n\nBut how does that align with the steps? \n\nWait, maybe the correct way is that each 0 needs to move past all the 1s that are to its left. So, for each 0, the number of 1s before it is the number of swaps it needs to make. \n\nBut why in the example, the first 0 has 1 1 before it, and the second 0 has 2 1s before it. So total swaps 3. But when I tried to simulate, I thought it was 5. Hmm, maybe I made a mistake in the simulation. \n\nLet me try again. Original array [1, 0, 1, 0]. \n\nStep 1: Swap 