 are equal. Wait, if the counts are equal, then the number of differing bits must be even. Because each swap changes the count of 0s and 1s by 0. So, if the total number of differing bits is even, then it\'s possible to pair them. So the minimum number of swaps is (number of differing bits) / 2.\n\nWait, but that\'s not necessarily true. For example, if the target is \'000111\' and the source is \'010111\', then the differing bits are at positions 1 and 2. So two differences, which would require 1 swap. But in that case, swapping positions 1 and 2 would fix both differences. So yes, the number of swaps is (number of differing bits) / 2.\n\nSo the steps would be:\n\n1. Check if the two strings have the same number of 0s and 1s. If not, return "Not Possible".\n2. Count the number of differing bits.\n3. If the number of differing bits is even, return (differing_bits) // 2.\n4. Otherwise, return "Not Possible".\n\nWait, but how to check if the counts are equal? For example, for the two strings, the count of 0s and 1s must be the same. So, for each string, count the number of 0s and 1s. If they are different, return "Not Possible".\n\nSo the code would look like:\n\ndef min_Swaps(s1, s2):\n    if len(s1) != len(s2):\n        return "Not Possible"\n    if s1.count(\'0\') != s2.count(\'0\') or s1.count(\'1\') != s2.count(\'1\'):\n        return "Not Possible"\n    # Now, count the number of differing bits\n    diff = sum(1 for a, b in zip(s1, s2) if a != b)\n    if diff % 2 != 0:\n        return "Not Possible"\n    return diff // 2\n\nWait, but what if the strings are of the same length? Well, the problem says they are binary strings, so they must have the same length. So the first check is to ensure that their lengths are the same. But the problem says that the input is two binary strings, so perhaps they have the same length. But the code should handle that.\n\nBut according to the problem statement, the function is given two binary strings. So the code can assume that t