1.5 → returns 1.5.\n\nSo the function seems correct.\n\nNow, the user wants the code written in solution.py, which is a new file. But the problem is just to write the function. So the answer would be the function definition.\n\nBut the user also mentioned that the code must adhere to company-mandated coding standards. For example, avoid side effects, use Pythonic solutions, compose functions.\n\nSo the function is straightforward. The code is simple and doesn't have side effects. The list comprehension isn't needed here, but perhaps the problem expects a list comprehension for some reason. But in this case, the function is a single line.\n\nSo the final answer is the function as written.\n</think>\n\n<answer>\ndef slope(x1, y1, x2, y2):\n    if x2 == x1:\n        return float('inf')\n    return (y2 - y1) / (x2 - x1)\n</answer>"
